```
[HIGH] Resilience policy composition creates retry storms with multiplicative timeout
File: /Users/robinsonchan/seatsniper/src/adapters/base/circuit-breaker.ts:202-205
```

The policy composition is `timeout -> retry -> circuitBreaker -> bulkhead`. This means the single outer timeout of 10 seconds wraps *all* retry attempts. With 3 retries and exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s delays), the total time needed is approximately 17 seconds minimum (3 requests + backoff waits), but the outer timeout is only 10 seconds. The timeout will frequently fire *during* the retry sequence, causing `TaskCancelledError` that masks the actual API error.

```typescript
// FIX: Either move timeout inside retries (per-attempt timeout)
// or increase the outer timeout to accommodate retries.

// Option A: Per-attempt timeout (recommended):
const combinedPolicy = wrap(retryPolicy, timeoutPolicy, breaker, bulkheadPolicy);
// This retries on timeout, with circuit breaker tracking timeout failures.

// Option B: Increase outer timeout:
// timeoutMs should be: (maxRetryAttempts * requestTimeout) + total backoff delay
// e.g., 3 * 10_000 + (1000 + 2000 + 4000) = 37_000ms
```

---

```
[HIGH] Rate limiter acquire() can go negative, allowing more requests than the limit
File: /Users/robinsonchan/seatsniper/src/utils/rate-limiter.ts:80-87
```

In `acquire()`, when a token is not immediately available, the method calculates a wait time and schedules a `setTimeout`. When the timer fires, it calls `this.refill()` then `this.tokens -= 1` without re-checking if `this.tokens >= 1`. If multiple callers are waiting concurrently, all of them schedule timers that fire around the same time, and all subtract from the token pool. This causes `this.tokens` to go negative, effectively allowing more requests than permitted.

```typescript
// CURRENT (line 80-87):
return new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    this.refill();
    this.tokens -= 1;
    resolve();
    this.processQueue();
  }, waitTime);
});

// FIX: Use a proper queue-based approach:
return new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
  this.waitQueue.push(resolve);
  setTimeout(() => {
    this.processQueue();
  }, waitTime);
});
```

Note: the existing `waitQueue` field (line 30) is declared but `acquire()` never actually uses it. The `processQueue` method (line 93-98) processes the queue but nothing ever enqueues into it.

